; `effect(div)` names an effect nothing in the compiler infers, so no
; body can ever support the claim: it is AX3110, an error, and not
; AX3010, whose message would accuse the body of missing something the
; compiler never looks for.
;
; Inferring it is not the answer, and that is measured rather than
; assumed: the cheapest sound rule - a self-call, or any `while` -
; marks 65% of this compiler divergent and calls the syntax zoo's own
; counted loop non-terminating.
;
; `diverges` really does not return, and is still refused: the walk
; does not infer divergence for a body that HAS it either. `apply`
; calls its parameter, and an open row buys the claim nothing, since no
; callback can supply what nothing produces.
;
; `noted` keeps the same intent in a key of its own, which no check
; reads, and draws nothing. `honest` is the control: a claim naming an
; effect that HAS a producer stays AX3010 when the body does not
; support it.
(:: quiet (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(div)
(fn (quiet n)
  n)

(:: diverges (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(div)
(fn (diverges n)
  (diverges n))

(:: apply (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(div)
(fn (apply k n)
  (k n))

(:: noted (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:my:div
(fn (noted n)
  (noted n))

(:: honest (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(mut)
(fn (honest n)
  n)

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (quiet 1))
